WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.630
- Have you ever driven a car?

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.530
Then you've broken the law.

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:05.310
Have you ever sold anything on eBay?

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.660
Then you've broken the law.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.990
Have you ever shared
your Netflix password?

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:10.300
Then you've broken the law.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.550
Yes, there are a lot of
laws in the United States,

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:17.150
and every single one of you
are dirty, filthy criminals

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.130
because you have broken the law

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:20.700
and you might not have known it.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.330
Studies show that the average
American breaks 473 laws

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:26.770
every single day.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:30.330
I just made that up, but it's
probably close to the truth

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.540
because there's so many
laws in the United States

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.160
that people just can't help
but break the law sometimes.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.770
So, let's talk about all of the ways

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.698
that you broke the law, but
probably didn't even know it.

00:00:40.698 --> 00:00:44.200
(cheerful music)

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.330
Now, everyone knows that
in many jurisdictions

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.210
it's illegal to gamble, and
a lot of people do it anyway,

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:50.730
especially with friends.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.530
But what about your
weekly at-home poker game?

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.760
Is this really, truly illegal?

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.760
Well, local laws differ but generally,

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.930
if the host accepts money,
so-called "taking a rake,"

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.710
then yes, this is actually illegal.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.830
- I'm shocked, shocked,
to find that gambling

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.650
is going on in here.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:09.483
- You're winnings, sir.

00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:10.600
- Oh, thank you very much.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.750
Everybody out at once.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.360
- And often, in many places,

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.890
in order for a game to
be considered gambling,

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.990
it must involve a prize or consideration

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.120
and be determined mostly by chance.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.350
If there's no consideration

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.460
or the game is deemed a game of skill,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.440
then that game will be
legal in most jurisdictions.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.140
So, what is poker?

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.290
Isn't it arguably a game of skill?

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:30.880
Well, defendants have made this argument

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.070
with limited success.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.180
In Colorado, back in 2009,

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.780
Kevin Raley started a poker league

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.840
where the league kept 10% of
the entrance fee for expenses

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.460
to pay for the dealers who
were running the games.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.390
The Colorado Bureau of Investigation did

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.750
an undercover investigation
and arrested five people

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.080
for playing Texas hold'em.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.790
Raley was charged with illegal gambling,

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.417
but a jury acquitted him
after a professor testified

00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:54.220
that poker was a game
of skill, not chance.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.720
Unfortunately for Raley, the
Court of Appeals disagreed

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.070
that poker was mostly
about skill, not chance,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.740
and the Appeals Court ruled
that the expert testimony

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:02.620
should have been excluded.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.280
Now, the Colorado Supreme
Court has declined

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:05.290
to hear Raley's appeal.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.330
But the bottom line is that
if your local poker host

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.494
or dealer is taking some
sort of consideration,

00:02:12.494 --> 00:02:13.520
(dramatic music)

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.790
then that might be an illegal game

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.270
and you might have broken
the law by participating.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.840
Now, when you've gone on the internet,

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.690
have you ever pretended to
be something that you aren't?

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.810
Well, you might have broken the law.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.080
Catfishing of the computer kind

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.220
is defined as the
process of luring someone

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.320
into a relationship by means
of a fictional online persona.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.320
And if you've ever seen MTV's "Catfish,"

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:34.240
then you know the drill.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.450
Someone creates a
fictitious online profile,

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.830
then tricks someone else into
falling in love with them.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.710
- You can't possibly think
it's still Katy Perry.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:42.543
- I do.

00:02:42.543 --> 00:02:45.270
- Can you just pop his bubble for a second

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.260
and tell him that he has not
been talking to Katy Perry?

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.770
- The typical catfish situation

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.430
includes stealing someone else's pictures,

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.310
often they belong to
some unsuspecting model,

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.540
and texting the target with false promises

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:57.880
of everlasting love.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.180
- The one problem is he
doesn't have a cell phone,

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.600
or a landline, or a car,

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.810
or any people who know him besides me.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.960
- Now, creating fake online profiles

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.680
definitely violates the terms and services

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.510
of most dating and social media sites,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:13.900
such as OkCupid and Facebook.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.930
But catfishing in and of itself
is not necessarily illegal.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.960
However, some of the catfish's activities

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.440
can violate criminal law.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.770
If the purpose of the catfishing
is to get money and gifts,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.470
then this can be prosecuted as fraud.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.820
If the catfish uses personal
information about their target

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.120
to get loans or credit cards,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.000
this is probably identity theft.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.210
If the fake identity
is used to lure minors

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.670
into a relationship, then that
act could definitely violate

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:37.860
child protection laws.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.680
And although breaking U.S. copyright laws

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.640
won't land you in jail necessarily,

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.110
using someone else's photographs

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:44.490
might be copyright infringement,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.970
and the Copyright Act allows the victim

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.830
to collect actual and statutory damages

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:48.810
for that infringement.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.810
And if you decided to pass
yourself off as a model

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:52.650
using images you found on Google,

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.420
you could get in even bigger trouble

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.320
because damages for a licensed image

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.850
could be up to $150,000 per photo,

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.130
especially if the
misrepresentation was willful.

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.100
So, yeah, don't go out
there catfishing people,

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.610
even if you're trying to
become the next Chris Hansen.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:06.607
- I'm Chris Hansen.

00:04:06.607 --> 00:04:07.440
- No, you're not.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.273
- Yes, I am.

00:04:08.273 --> 00:04:09.597
- No you're not.
- Yes, I am.

00:04:09.597 --> 00:04:11.480
- Now, people love tiny houses,

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.080
but if you're living in a tiny house,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.520
you might be breaking the law.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.860
Building tiny houses is
something of a trend in the U.S.,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.640
but these stylish Keebler
houses aren't always kosher

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.080
in the eyes of the law.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.110
Now, the Department of Housing
and Urban Development's

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.190
newest regulations give
RVs and tiny houses

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.120
a little more breathing
room under the law,

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.710
but the regs also say that tiny
houses which rest on a skid

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:34.110
or foundation, or are
over 400 square feet,

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.380
must meet HUD's manufactured
housing standards

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:37.410
to be considered legal.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.900
That said, whether your tiny home is legal

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.190
depends almost entirely
on how your local area

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.660
classifies tiny homes.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:46.360
Some cities ban homes
under 400 square feet,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.260
while others ban them
if they are moveable.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.140
Yes, that's right, lots
of cities and states

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.990
love to fight high housing costs

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.550
by making it harder to build more housing.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:55.780
But living in a tiny house

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.130
can also have criminal consequences

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.580
because a court can conclude
that your mobile vehicle

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.720
isn't a home at all for purposes
of the Fourth Amendment.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.260
In California versus Carney,
the Supreme Court ruled

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.380
that the warrantless
search of a motor home

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:07.970
did not violate the Fourth
Amendment's guarantee

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:09.640
against warrantless searches and seizures.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.910
Why? Because the court
considered it a vehicle.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.280
The Supreme Court has
said that the Constitution

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.220
requires a lesser degree of
protection for motor vehicles

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.380
because they can move quickly

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.090
before a warrant can be obtained.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.410
And in that particular
case, the man's motor home

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.910
wasn't in a residential area
and was licensed as a vehicle.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.410
So, you might get it both ways.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:29.320
You might be illegally
living in a tiny house

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.260
and also not receive all kinds
of constitutional protections

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.340
afforded for actual houses.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:37.260
Have you ever warmed up
your car before driving off?

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.200
Well, you might have broken the law.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.350
If you've ever lived above
the Mason-Dixon line,

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.100
then you're probably
familiar with the concept

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.750
of warming up the car.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.290
If the wind chill is hovering around

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:47.570
zero degrees Fahrenheit,

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.110
then it seems like a pretty
good idea to start the car,

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.480
turn on the heater, and let
the car idle for a while

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.060
before you actually climb in.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.730
That way you won't freeze to death.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:57.000
But not so fast.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.950
Many states have anti-idling laws.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.540
At least 25 states ban idling

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.880
because it contributes
to air pollution, duh.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.710
The American Transportation
Research Institute

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.842
has a full compendium
of state and local laws

00:06:06.842 --> 00:06:10.280
that prohibit idling before
you start driving away.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.430
And unsurprisingly, the
further north you go,

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:13.950
the more complicated the laws.

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:16.610
In Denver, for instance, you're
not supposed to idle the car

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:17.930
for more than five minutes an hour,

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:20.380
which can get you a fine of up to $1,000.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.500
But there are exceptions to
reflect the cold weather.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.830
If the average temperature
has been under 20 degrees

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.900
for 24 hours or it's less
than 10 degrees outside,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.380
you can heat up your car
for as long as you want.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:32.120
Your mileage may vary.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:34.410
Have you ever shared your Netflix password

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:35.590
with someone else?

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.730
Well, then you might have broken the law.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.370
Could sharing your Netflix password

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.720
actually violate federal law?

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:45.100
You bet it might possibly
theoretically do that.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.410
The Computer Fraud &amp; Abuse Act, or CFAA,

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.860
is a federal law that imposes
civil and criminal penalties

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.780
when a person accesses a
computer without authorization

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.040
or exceeding authorized access.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.220
Believe it or not, federal
courts cannot agree

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:58.940
on whether this means it's against the law

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.560
for you to share your password.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.690
In 2016, a split Ninth
Circuit Court of Appeals

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:05.210
ruled that these words mean
that a person cannot access

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:08.020
another person's account
using that person's password

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:09.230
when the person has revoked

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:10.810
the first person's access rights.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:12.690
Judge Reinhart, who
dissented in that case,

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.490
warned that a broad
interpretation of the CFAA's

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:17.727
in excess of authorization prong would,

00:07:17.727 --> 00:07:20.960
"Criminalize the ordinary
conduct of millions of citizens."

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.010
And yes, that means you.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.700
Other circuits have
ruled in similar cases,

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.990
but there is no consensus
view on when the person's use

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:29.150
of someone else's password is illegal.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.450
The defendant from the Ninth Circuit case

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.130
asked the Supreme Court
to resolve the issue,

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.070
but the U.S. Solicitor
General filed a brief

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:34.903
for the government saying

00:07:34.903 --> 00:07:36.570
that there wasn't really a circuit split

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:39.370
and that the issue was narrow
whether someone could continue

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:41.860
using a password after the
person revoked their permission.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.270
The Supreme Court denied cert.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.103
So, what now?

00:07:44.103 --> 00:07:47.260
Many commentators have said
that the CFAA is way too broad

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.360
and criminalizes tons and tons of activity

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:50.950
that should not be considered illegal.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.200
And the Supreme Court
has in fact curtailed

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.010
some of the aspects of the CFAA

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:57.690
with respect to police officers
and government officials.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:00.500
But the question of sharing your password

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.150
remains open at this time.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.560
So, what do you do now?

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.120
Well, you're gonna have to ask yourself

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.400
whether watching "Bridgerton"
is worth going to jail over.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:09.900
And then back to gambling

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.720
because if you've ever bet on sports,

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.690
then you might have broken the law.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:15.080
The rules against gambling on sports

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:16.790
were relaxed after the
Supreme Court's decision

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:18.620
in Murphy versus the NCAA.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.580
This decision paved the way for states

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:21.670
to permit sports betting,

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:25.110
but there are still federal
laws disallowing sports betting,

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.890
and this invites all
kinds of police overreach.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:31.260
For example, the Illegal
Gambling Business Act of 1970

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.330
was passed to target organized crime.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.530
And under that law, a
gambling business is illegal

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:39.410
if they exceed over $2,000 of
gross revenue in a single day.

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:41.180
And this law is broad
enough to implicate people

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:43.460
who aren't part of any
organized crime organizations.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.610
So if you or your friends
violate some local laws

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:47.980
against sports gambling
and wager more than $2,000

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.350
in a single day, the cops can bust you.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.090
And in a particularly tragic case,

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:56.000
a Virginia detective overheard
an optometrist, Sal Culosi,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.310
talking about betting on
college football games

00:08:58.310 --> 00:08:59.143
with his friends.

00:08:59.143 --> 00:09:01.490
The detective then befriended Culosi,

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:03.720
eventually convincing him
to bet more than $2,000

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:04.553
in a single day.

00:09:04.553 --> 00:09:07.680
Then the police brought a
SWAT team to Culosi's house

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.450
and killed him in that raid.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.880
Have you ever used someone's
Wi-Fi without permission?

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.160
Well, you might have broken the law.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:16.640
Now, not all U.S.
jurisdictions make it illegal

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:18.840
to steal Wi-Fi, but in Sparta, Michigan,

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.130
a man was arrested for
using a cafe's Wi-Fi

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.600
to check his email while
sitting in his car.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:25.540
The cafe owner didn't know it was illegal

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:28.740
nor did the man, but a cop
watched him do this every day

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.280
and decided it was suspicious.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:31.967
Then he found the law that made,

00:09:31.967 --> 00:09:34.210
"Unauthorized computer access," a crime.

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:36.800
So not only are there
issues on a federal level

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.790
with the CFAA, but
there are also all kinds

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:42.430
of local laws that might make
unauthorized computer access

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:45.230
a crime, even if there aren't any victims

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:47.400
in stealing that person's Wi-Fi.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.770
Have you ever purchased
spray paint or markers?

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.750
Well, you might have broken the law.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.680
Some states and cities
really hate graffiti.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.920
Despite the fact that most
people who buy spray paint

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:57.670
have no intention of breaking the law,

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.640
the authorities have decided
that they need to stop

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:01.550
a certain group of people
from buying spray paint,

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:03.400
and those people are minors.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.130
Now, laws banning miners
from buying paint and markers

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.470
originated in the early
1990s when disaffected youth

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:12.100
were wearing flannel, listening
to Public Enemy and Nirvana,

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:13.960
and hanging out around skate parks.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:15.690
Ah, the horror!

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.950
But young people listening
to rap and grunge

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:19.900
freaked out city officials around America

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:21.300
who were absolutely certain

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.900
that these minors were
defacing public property.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.770
Therefore, in California,

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:28.070
it's illegal for any person
under the age of 18 years

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.770
to purchase etching cream or
an aerosol container of paint

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.710
that is capable of defacing property.

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:36.360
Penalties include up to 100
hours of community service

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.130
enforced over a 90-day period.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:39.850
And if you own a business
that sells paint,

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:41.540
you're also a target of these laws.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:44.047
Quote, "Every retailer
selling or offering for sale

00:10:44.047 --> 00:10:45.290
"in the state etching cream,"

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:46.167
or whatever the hell that is,

00:10:46.167 --> 00:10:49.157
"or aerosol containers of paint
capable of defacing property

00:10:49.157 --> 00:10:52.327
"shall post in a conspicuous
place a sign in letters

00:10:52.327 --> 00:10:54.687
"at least three-eights
of an inch high stating:

00:10:54.687 --> 00:10:57.427
"'Any person who maliciously defaces real

00:10:57.427 --> 00:11:00.257
"or personal property with
etching cream or paint

00:11:00.257 --> 00:11:01.697
"is guilty of vandalism,

00:11:01.697 --> 00:11:04.480
"which is punishable by a
fine, imprisonment, or both.'"

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.910
These laws really haven't
had much an impact

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.010
on the presence of graffiti,

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.360
and they've been challenged
by people impacted by the law.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.730
Fashion designer Mark Ecko
actually sued New York City

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:15.800
on behalf of college
and high school students

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.070
who he claimed had their
First Amendment rights

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:19.530
infringed upon by the city's law,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.640
which banned the purchase
of certain materials

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.170
to people under the age of 21.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:26.130
The Second Circuit ruled
that the law was overly broad

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:27.540
and restrictive because it barred youths

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:30.880
from even possessing markers
or spray paint in public

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:33.330
or carrying the materials
on public transportation

00:11:33.330 --> 00:11:34.163
or in a school.

00:11:34.163 --> 00:11:36.580
The law as written hindered
young adults' access

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:37.850
to materials that they needed

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:39.680
for lawful artistic expression.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:42.500
Man, parents, they just don't understand.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:45.160
Now, have you ever sold anything on eBay

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.810
and not reported your income?

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.770
If so, you have broken the law.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:52.070
If you sell things online on
eBay or Poshmark or Craigslist,

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:53.970
you have to report those profits as income

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:55.030
on your tax return.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:56.700
Now, the first question you need to answer

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.250
is whether your eBay habit
is a business or a hobby.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.340
If you get a steady
profit from your sales,

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:03.530
the IRS will probably
classify this as a business,

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.350
but if you're only an occasional seller,

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:07.060
the IRS considers it a hobby.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:09.950
And in either instance, the
income needs to be reported.

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:11.690
But if your sales are of a business,

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:14.490
then you have the option
of deducting more expenses.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.650
Though, as usual, when
the word IRS is used,

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:17.790
please talk to an accountant

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:20.270
before you do absolutely anything at all.

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:22.320
Have you ever sat down on the sidewalk?

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:25.010
Well, then you have
probably broken the law.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.660
In about 53% of American cities,
you've broken an ordinance

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:31.080
against sitting or lying down in public.

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:33.260
These are known as sit-lie ordinances.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:34.970
It's controversial to ban people

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:36.400
from hanging out on a sidewalk.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.450
Critics of these laws obviously point out

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.830
that these are basically
designed to attack people

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.630
